Date: Fri, 5 Mar 1993 10:35:21 -0500 (EST)
From: Edward J OConnell <ejo@world.std.com>
Subject: PHIL/ECON/NANO/NAIVE: Post Scarcity Manifesto

A POST SCARCITY MANIFESTO: Version 2.0

WARNING: Politically, I have at times in the past considered
myself a democratic socialist. My current thinking is more
anarchic. It is not, however, capitalist. So most of you should
hit-N now. I'm 'economically naive.'  (i.e Not Libertarian.)

I said, hit-N now. This is what they do in alt.sex stories, so
folks don't accidentally read child/rape/mutilation fantasy's, and
I whole heartedly approve.

I'm a science fiction writer (First pro story in this months
Aboriginal SF) and I find this list very useful, but I got tired
of lurking, and thought I'd air some of my views.

I'll understand if people want me off the list, afterwards.
Although I rather hope I'm just ignored.

There is much I like about this list; much I dislike. It is an
uncomfortable situation for me. I don't see all the ideas here as
neatly wrapped up in one package.

I find myself picking through Extropianism, taking what I'm
interested in, leaving what I'm not. I hope that eventually there
will be a filter for the list so that I can get what I want,
without having to listen to a lot of whining about creeping
socialism in a country that has been ruled by right wing pinheads
for the last decade.

(It's the drug war and the military industrial complex that have
impoverished this country; not leftist/statist ideology, IMO.)

***

THE ESSAY:

I'm thinking now of the ultimate irony, one about which I would
someday like to write a SF novel, about the freedoms of the
post-scarcity age, and how we will get there. My central tenet is
this: no one will sell us freedom.

We will steal it.

Freedom (TM) will be invented and patented by the FreeMarket (TM),
the loose affiliation of oligopolies that currently own the planet
Earth and its citizens. It will be manufactured in the form of
self replicating complexes of nanomachines--with copy protection
up the ying-yang.

Because scarcity culture will fight the coming of the post
scarcity culture tooth and nail. This reminds me of a string on
Extropians, in which many worried about what they would need to
own to stay on top in a post scarcity world. What indeed, could
one own? What can you sell to the man who has everything? What
could a caveman own, that a twentieth century man could possibly
care about? His collections of sharpened flints? Animals furs?
Cave paintings? Certainly all will have some value.
Anthropological only, of course.

The evolution of the software industry may mimic the path that the
post scarcity society culture will follow. Early on, expect
incredible attempts to hold on to the powers of replication, to
hide it in ciphers, to create unbreakable locks and keys. Matched
by similar ingenuity, again market driven, to break the locks,
distribute the keys, to steal access to the new technologies.

Because nanotechnology is the ultimate gift that keeps on giving.

Willy Wonka referred to nanotechnology as the Everlasting
Gobstopper--but even as a child, I realized that no corporation
could ever market such a product. Wonka himself presented the
gobstopper as an almost altruistic gift, for small children with
'little spending money'...

Ahh, there's that word. Altruism!

This copy protection phase will be followed, perhaps, by a period
of openness as increased wealth creates the laxness we now see in
the software industry. Many LAN managers now have the power, more
or less, to decide how much they want to pay for software, by
deciding how honest they want to be. How many copies of MS Word
will I buy for my ten machines? Two or three seems like a good
idea. We'll have few extra sets of documentation, and, to be
honest, I don't think MS needs any more than 1500 dollars from my
business for its cumbersome product.

And Bill Gates isn't really hurting, is he? And won't hurt, into
the foreseeable future. (footnote 1)

Because the software industry realized that copy protection was a
complete pain in the ass. It prevented people from taking test
runs and kicking the tires. It may have actually hurt sales in an
environment where some products abandoned it for ease of use. And
ultimately, what the hell did it matter, that the product was
being stolen all over hell and back? They were *still* making
money hand over fist, with profit margins that the manufacturing
sector could only imagine in its wildest, drug induced fantasies.

Intuitively, we understand this, regardless of how much the
Coalition against Software Piracy (or whatever the hell the copy
cops are calling themselves now.) try to make us believe that by
copying something we're stealing it. Of course we're not. Stealing
is when I take from someone, *and they no longer have.* If I take
from someone, and they still have, then we have *shared.*

Information wants to be free.

After the period of laxness, expect the harrowing Last Gasp, as
the FreeMarket(TM) begins to realize that its about to be replaced
by a new, commodity rich environment that is truly *Free.* After
all, is there any current market value for air? Gravity?
*Anything* that is truly taken for granted?

Because believe me, after the development and liberation  of
robust replicators, a lot is going to be taken for granted.

The New Order, of Cheats Living on the Wealth of Others (TM),
those who have liberated replicating technology, may be attacked
before their philosophies can spread to the public at large, from
whom corporations have always harvested their wealth, and whom
they feel they own. Expect a big, nasty struggle. Perhaps, gray
goo. It could very well destroy the entire genome of this planet,
but that's apocalypse thinking, so lets not go down that reality
tunnel....  Maybe this is the thousand year reign of the anti
Christ. To live in a culture that could have plenty, but that
chooses to lock it up for the enjoyment of the privileged, who get
a kick out of having while other's have not...

I must admit, I have hard time imaging what the post scarcity
world will be like, but this isn't surprising. We can't see beyond
scarcity's horizon, because we're *damaged* by it, limited by its
impact on our minds and lives. Perhaps we'll be able to transcend
this kind of thinking in a century or two.  I hope so.

We see the generational differences in our grandparents, scarred
by the Great Depression. In our parents, who built bomb shelters
to hide from the Russians, who laid in stocks of canned goods and
bottled water. A generation shocked speechless by the thought of
throwing away food; who saved for a rainy day, because they
couldn't get the memory of the last deluge out of their minds.
Jerzy Kozinsky, holocaust survivor, for years couldn't enter a
room or building with planning escape routes.

It's hard to imagine ourselves so distorted. But we are.

You cannot turn on the news, look at the images of starving
children, their stomachs bloated with gas, their limbs like
blackened twigs, and be unaffected. You cannot walk by walk by
beggar after beggar after beggar, dirt smeared people living in
the streets without being damaged. Compassion is quickly eroded;
someone is to blame, and it's either us, or them. Either way,
depersonalization, objectification, a certain mental sorting takes
place.  It is ultimately destructive to humanity. This sorting may
be the basis for all coercion and injustice. It is codified into
law in the new testament, as Jesus's saying "the poor ye shall
have with ye always." So don't even think of eliminating scarcity.
Because you can't. IMO, may so called Rationalist Objectivist
Libertarian Cyronicists have taken this two thousand year old
nonsense to heart.

I don't believe in Jesus, and I don't believe in his modern
disciples of poverty, either. The market as we know it, (the Work
In My Factory Or Live On The Street and Eat The Government Cheese
Market,) will come to an end; it was a lifeboat, created by
population pressure, that carried us between free living as hunter
gatherers to free living as citizens of nanoculture in a world of
abundance we cannot truly imagine. A world beyond scarcity.

I hope to live to see it. TrueFreedom--a culture in which human
scale intelligence is not used for tasks better suited to
insectminded automata--(I've worked in factories. Can you tell?)
in which every entity joyfully creates for the pure joy of
creation, or consumes for the simple pleasure of consumption, in
an world in which there is always More Than Enough To Go Around.
(tm)

I'm being frozen when and if I die, so that hopefully I can see
how it all turns out. (I'm in the final stages of signup with
Alcor.) Although I don't think it will be a FreeMarket(TM) culture
that reanimates usQIn the world that resurrects us, nobody will
care much about our collections of flints and animal furs.

Except the anthropologists.

***

POSTSCRIPT: It is my conviction that ultimately, all belief
systems exist primarily to assuage the consciences of those that
hold them. As an artist and writer in a world where those things
are currently in great oversupply, I haven't been all that big a
fan of the FreeMarket. (Although I'm trying like hell to be
noticed in the SF marketplace, and am currently inordinately proud
of my first pro sale...)

God knows, if I really loved writing C-code, and was making a
hundred K yearly, I'd probably think the market was fair--DAMN
fair! :) Which is the Extropian position, the Conservative
position, the Capitalist position...

I'm hoping that technology will render a lot of these debates
moot.

If the future is indeed, as Perry has it, just a handful of
Jupiter sized intellects warring/trading for atoms in the
FreeMarket of the future, then I'm wrong, and who cares? That
doesn't sound like all that much fun to me anyway, and as my
friend and partner in ThoughCrime Ron Evans says, "I can always
die later." And maybe I will.

But I'm for hacking the future, rigging it for a diversity of
intellects as long as that's feasible, and for having a good time.
This seems to me not an unreasonable thing for us to do. Call it
lingering humanism. Even in Rudy Rucker's novels the bopper AI's
have a warm spot in their silicon hearts for us, their meat
predecessors. They like us and want to boogie with us, at least
for awhile.

I have a friend who is getting a Ph.D in biology. She gets no end
of pleasure from studying plankton. Perhaps that's the future of
human scale intellect, and our diverse biosphere...

As A-life for Big Brains!

I suppose it's just as likely the Jupiter Brains eventually will
devour and digest all us virus-sized, socialist-descended bacteria
minds. But until then, and for as long as we can get away with it,
I want to party as a nanobushmen, outlaw phreak, VONU nanoware
codebreaker.

Slack, at long last Slack!

*********************************** 
(1) The software piracy issue
is an uncomfortable one for me, as I do still have some
compunctions about 'stealing.' However, every programmer I've ever
known has bootlegged software gleefully; every association of computer
professionals I've ever worked with, has, at one point or another,
'let their hair down' and swapped software like mad. This is with
people who make their livings writing code.

I have a friend who writes code for the German Stock Exchange,
politically conservative, and I mentioned to him that I'd actually
gone out and bought some software. He told me he had 'a moral
objection' to paying for software for personal use.

I see these phenomena as an emerging ethical standard that takes
into account, 'stealing is when I take and you no longer have.'
Doubtless, some Libertarians who *never* pirate anything ( :))will
see it as evidence of creeping leftist brainrot.

As they will see this entire essay, come to think of it.

Oh well.

Jay




