   From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu

   The latest of these is the claim that we oppose "fairness," and
   want it abolished.

   Fairness is synomymous with justice.  I love justice.  I expect that many
   but not all of the rest of us do, too.  I seek a more just society.  Thus,
   my belief in libertarianism follows from my belief in fairness: I seek
   maximum equal liberty for all because I think it just.

There is also a concept of "fairness" which revolves around the idea
that competition should be "managed" or "regulated" so that one
participant doesn't "exploit" another.  As far as I can tell,
libertarianism opposes all such ideas.

Taken to the case of the supermarket check-out line, when a new line
opens up, it is an opportunity.  In a properly competitive world, the
person who observes the opportunity and acts on it first should get
the benefit of the opportunity.  In a world with "regulated"
competition, the benefit will be handed out to whomever is "most
deserving" of it, in this case, whoever has been waiting longest in
line.  That's like saying that frontier land should be owned by
whomever has spent the most effort to get there, not who has gotten
there first.

If you really want unregulated, dog-eat-dog competition in the market,
why not in the supermarket?

Dale

