   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   Why do you think that having things privately owned would eliminate
   people's rights?

Because there are many freedoms which seem to be opposed by solid
majorities of the population.  If every city block were autonomous,
what fraction of them would allow prostitution, drug sales,
unrestricted gun ownership, etc.?  Would there even be enough of them
that I could carry my gun from the store to my house?

And a lot of people and organizations have no attitude of
live-and-let-live toward other people.  Companies *do* give their
employees drug tests, not only to make sure they don't use drugs on
the job, but to make sure they don't use them at all.  Homosexuals are
still suffer assault and job discrimination fairly frequently.

And there is no real certainty that these restrictions wouldn't become
very widespread, even in a really decentralized world.  Odd movements
against various freedoms seem to catch on and spread.  (Meme
propagation?)  Around the turn of the century, the "Progressive
movement" included a wide-spread campaign against prostitution and
masturbation(!), which resulted it prostitution being outlawed in all
50 states.  As far as I know, there was no central authority that
forced this change; it was some sort of wide-spread social movement.
The War On Drugs seems to be operating in a similar fashion -- unlike
motorcycle helmet laws and the like, the states seem to be quite
willing to sign on.

Even more bizarrely, consider the spread of the "Martin Luther King
Day" meme.  Most of the states have approved MLK Day as a holiday.
Arizona didn't; presumably because it has very few blacks.  This
caused Arizona to become the target of a concerted boycott effort,
mostly associations refusing to have their conventions there.  (This
seems to be entirely LC.)  In the end, the loss of money convinced the
Arizona Legislature to pass MLK Day.

(From a memetics point of view, it would be interesting to discover
the underlying reason for success of these meme complexes.)

What I fear is that a society with an underlying libertarian
*structure*, if the people of it don't have libertarian *attitudes*,
will rapidly come to resemble our present society, in terms of the
freedoms one is allowed to exercise in practice.  It seems that
large-scale commons is one way to avoid this bind.

Without that, it seems that one might live in a world like the state
of Massachusetts, every square inch of which is part of one town or
another.  If 90% of those towns decide to restrict gun ownership, in
what way would it be a libertarian society?

Dale

