Subject: META/D-FENS: Defending Ourselves, and Flames

Tim May wonders:

> So why, in my own way, do I continue to live near the edge? The surge of
> adrenaline in poking a stick in Big Brother's eye? Nietzsche's "Live
> dangerously"?

The short answer might very well be "Yes, that's about right."

Rationality (even hyper-rationality) is not an end, it's a means.
Allen Newell's Principle of Rationality states "A person acts so
as to attain his goals through rational action, given the
structure of the task and his inputs of information and bounded
by limitations on his knowledge and processing ability".

So, is "the longest life possible" a reasonable goal for an
Extropian?  Yes, but not the ONLY goal!  Other goals, perhaps
involving experiential quality, acquisition of knowledge and
power, interaction with other sentient beings, etc, must figure
in the rational behavior equation.

A statistical advantage of a few months' lifespan is perhaps
not worth the reduction in "quality" (read: goal achievement)
required to attain it.

The BIG question is:  Which of those goals are "worthy", and
where do they come from?

derek
