Date: Fri, 5 Mar 93 10:42:15 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: Crypto-Lists

Garrett Goebel asks about encrypted lists, use of anonymous remailers, etc.
I'll spend a few minutes updating you folks on what's been going on in the
crypto arena in general and the "Cypherpunks" arena in particular. (BTW, I
didn't coin the term "cypherpunks," but it seems popular in some
quarters--"Wired" is about to have a cover story--so they tell me--on us.)

>Nick, myself, and a couple others have brought up discussion about
>encrypting mailing lists... Tim, and other Cypherpunks:  Is something
>like this possible?

Yes. But consider some important points:

* the encryption software, most likely PGP but possibly RIPEM, is not
operational for everyone: some platforms won't run it, some folks won't
bother, some UNIX sites may not allow it, etc.

* if the entire list shares the _same_ key, to decrypt the files, then
what's the point?

* on the other hand, if each recipient get his own customized encrypted
file, this is a fair amount of work for the list manager (who would have to
encrypt hundreds of messages for each transmission).

* unless the subscriber qualification process (the bona fides, as it were)
is tightened up, suspicious folks could still get a subscription--the
encryption would have been for naught.


>I understand that the list administration is currently working on 
>some software (unless I dreamt it) to help in filtering the list of
>excess noise... and helping with organization.  Here are some 
>uneducated ideas on ways to add protection.
>
>o  add and ANON: prefix that will erase all traces of the sender 
>   from the post.

This is possible by using one of the various anonymous remailers, assuming
the list software will let "outsiders" onto the list (as it did with the
CompuSlave account of the White House).

>o  sending post in through anonomous remailers

The list software would have to allow these unknown sites through.

>o  setting up the list to accept PGP encrypted posts
>   -list members for be given a public key to post to the list
>   -and a private key to read from the list
>   
>1) Anonomous posting would protect identities... but could let
>   flames go out of control

Yes, but the NetNews experience has been mixed: flames have not increased
dramatically since the recent surge in anonyomous postings.

>2) Anonomous remailers could protect identities... and we could
>   control flames to a limited extent by bringing action against
>   flames made from certain remailer addresses ie. 1834anon@anon.penet.fi
>
>3) Sure, anyone who penetrated the list could spy in... but all
>   our back files sitting on computers around the country would be
>   unreadable to those without the keys.

Again, if _we_ can read the back files, so can _they_.

(My alarm yesterday was not some ignorant realization that perhaps Feds and
others could gain access to the list postings, but alarm that we were
"handing the list to them on a silver plate." Sending postings openly to a
branch of the government has a different legal standing than if those
branches gain access through third parties, through deception, and through
surveillance. This was my point.)

Those interested in crypto issues should subscribe to the Cypherpunks list
be sending a request to "cypherpunks-request@toad.com".

-Tim May

